The following is the announcement of the Ministry of Justice: The 41st ambulance donated by Samdech Techo and Samdech Kittipritthbandit was handed over to the health center in Tram Kak district, Takeo province.
(Takeo): The 41st ambulance donated by Samdech Techo Hun Sen, Prime Minister of Cambodia and Samdech Kittipritthbandit Bun Rany Hun Sen, on August 28, 2020, was handed over to Ang Ta Som Commune Health Center, Tram Kak District, Takeo Province through Mr. Oknha Dr. Chea Ratana, personal advisor to Samdech Techo Hun Sen and Oknha Ly Hiek Ratana.
Oknha Dr. Chea Ratana said that due to the need for an ambulance at the Tram Kak District Referral Hospital, "We must help, no matter in which district and region of the province, especially in this district, there are many people, the need for services. "Health, despite traffic restrictions, but accidents still happen every day, so through this need we must help immediately."
Therefore, as citizens, we must be very careful in traveling to avoid traffic accidents that cause harm to both us and others and cause difficulties for families and society.
Oknha Dr. Chea Ratana continued, "As I am a personal advisor to Samdech Techo Hun Sen, the Prime Minister of Cambodia, and as a citizen and have little access to the generous people who like to do charity programs, we will work for Cambodia and "Our people will help fill any gaps to improve further."
On that occasion, Oknha Dr. Chea Ratana also thanked all the philanthropists who have been actively involved in humanitarian work in the past, especially this ambulance, with Mr. Oknha Sok Vanna helping to fund the purchase of the 41st ambulance. $ 8,000, and this is not the first time, and there are a series of car donations, a total of 69 units.
Meanwhile, the Director of Tram Kak District Referral Hospital, Takeo Province, Dr. Heng Thy expressed his deep gratitude for the precious gift of Samdech Techo Hun Sen and Samdech Kittiprittbandit Bun Rany Hun Sen through Oknha Dr. Chea Ratana.
He said that the donation of an ambulance is a means and an urgent need to send patients in Tram Kak district and the people in Takeo province to rescue in time and reduce the death rate. Patients. "In addition, in remote areas where there is no way to get to the hospital, and in line with the hospital here, the need."
At the same time, "In response to this precious gift, we are all committed to fulfilling our obligations and providing health services to the people with equity and quality. In addition, we are committed to maintaining Samdech Techo's ambulance. Hun Sen and Samdech Kittipritthbandit Bun Rany Hun Sen to be a legacy for the hospital and for the benefit of the people in Tram Kak district as a whole.
Mr. Kao Vaksel, Tram Kak District Governor, also said that this ambulance is a necessity in our district because Tram Kak district has a large population of up to 15 communes with a total population of 200,000 people, so the need is Large scope, especially ready to rescue some traffic accident victims.
On behalf of the people, Mr. Tram Kak District Governor would like to express his gratitude to Samdech Techo Hun Sen and Samdech Kittipritthbandit Bun Rany Hun Sen for providing this ambulance for the benefit of the people.
(Phnom Penh): The United States Agency for International Development (USAID) Labor Rights Program in Cambodia, implemented by the Solidarity Center, is supporting a workshop with 80 union representatives on improving road safety for Cambodian workers to promote the equity of Working conditions in the factory, including safety in commuting to work of workers.
The Asia Injury Prevention Foundation, a partner organization of the Solidarity Center, will hold its second workshop on August 27, 2020 to highlight the vision of traffic safety policy to protect the safety of factory workers on the road. As part of the program "Promoting traffic safety for Cambodian workers."
These visions include: providing training for co-truck drivers, encouraging motorcyclists to receive free helmets, and training on free motorcycle driving techniques. Charge workers, switch from common means of transportation by truck to safe passenger vehicles, and adopt and enforce traffic safety policies for implementation in factories across the country.
Over the last decade, the garment industry in Cambodia has grown significantly, but the safety of workers' travel remains a dangerous challenge. As a result, deaths and injuries remain high.
"Factory workers are a big part of the community," said Min Manvy, secretary of state at the Ministry of Public Works and Transport and secretary general of the National Road Safety Committee. "Their contribution to the nation and the economy should be given attention to welfare and security."
She said unions play an important role in protecting the interests of factory workers, who not only focus on "rights" in working conditions in the factory, but also in commuting. Therefore, there is a need for good cooperation between unions and stakeholders to improve travel safety and harmony and good operation in factories.
"We look forward to working with you to find a solution to ensure safe travel for workers," said Veena Reddy, Director of the United States Agency for International Development (USAID) Country Director for Cambodia. In the field of labor.
Kim Panha, Director of the Asian Injury Prevention Foundation in Cambodia, said: “Trade union leaders have an important role to play in advocating on behalf of their members to improve working conditions, including travel safety. Of workers and means of transportation as well.
"We hope that through this workshop, trade unions will be encouraged to raise the issue of travel and call on orders and factories to address the safety and well-being of workers," he said. "High priority."
