Minister Thong Khon: In this new context, tourism workers need to know how to use the internet and follow safety rules
(Phnom Penh): As the whole world is facing the crisis, Kovid-19 has forced people to change their habits, including tourism, has experienced a lot of changes, even the training of tourism professionals can not Adhere to the same standards as before, there is a need for better quality.
In this context, the Committee of Tourism Experts as well as the Ministry of Tourism of Cambodia proposed many new solutions from the meeting of the Committee of Tourism Experts (NCCC) for the first half of the year and the direction of implementation in the second year. 2020, held on the afternoon of August 27 at the Ministry of Tourism under the chairmanship of Mr. Thong Khon, Minister of Tourism.
At this meeting, many important points were raised, including the promotion of tourism professional training to be implemented more digitally or online. In addition, all tourism professional training standards must include knowledge of tourism safety rules, which is mandatory for tourism professionals from the context of the Kovid epidemic.
Other ASEAN member states have supported and considered Cambodia a leader in the implementation of the MRA, and they have also called on Cambodia to set standards for massage training. For this standard, Cambodia has prepared and implemented a series of successful training, and the Ministry of Tourism has already converted from a national standard to an ASEAN standard.
He said that all massage therapists must go through the training of ASEAN trainers who are recognized based on the qualifications and quality of ASEAN. Those who have already received individual skills training from ASEAN trainers, they want to work in any country as long as they are in the ASEAN region, but also in accordance with the labor laws of the host country and the sending country, that is, under Jurisdiction of the Ministry of Labor of each country.
In 2021, ASEAN will also introduce 32 MICE standards before new skills related to the organization of major events, major meetings, exhibitions and so on.
Therefore, the standard of training for ASEAN tourism professionals under the MRA will increase to 72 pre-specialties, including 32 hotel and travel service standards, 6 massage standards and 32 new MICE standards.
During the meeting, Minister Thong Khon emphasized the need to ensure that all training standards for tourism professionals, both old and new, a total of 70 subjects, must include standards for implementing tourism safety rules for tourism professionals. The nation and ASEAN are working in line with the current context of the Kovid-19 crisis and for the changing situation in the future.
The minister further suggested that for the training of tourism professionals in the context of Kovid disease, it is necessary to promote digital or online training because people in the world in this new context, whether they want to or not, they need to change their lifestyle. New Normal Life, and some of the communication that used to be practiced in the past, also need to be remotely implemented through this online system.
"We need to encourage our tourism professionals to think that working or learning online is a normal habit that everyone should use," he said. This is to push this online knowledge to all human resources in Cambodia's tourism sector, and by then we will achieve real quality human resources.
