(Phnom Penh): Speaking on the occasion of the Royal Government Spokesperson's Unit held a press conference on "Blood Donation to Save Lives" this morning, August 27, 2020, Mr. Sok Po, President of the National Blood Transfusion Center, said that the effects of the crisis Kovid 19 greatly reduces the number of people who donate blood.
Mr. Sok Po said that the 19-crisis Kovid crisis has affected the whole world in all areas. In addition, donating blood in Cambodia has had a significant impact, as volunteers for free blood donation have had to take measures to maintain respect for social gaps, individual gaps and other hygiene issues, making them feel too complicated. They are also afraid of infecting 19 Kovid.
"Last year, there were 12,000 volunteers, halved," he said. So there are less than 10,000 volunteers for 2020. "However, we have been working hard in recent months to restore the possibility of collecting more blood."
Mr. Sok Po added that the amount of blood received from voluntary donations is not enough to supply yet, and according to international standards, if the demand can be met until blood donations from 1% of the population. Total population. Of that, for Cambodia, out of 16 million people, there must be about 160,000 or more to have enough blood.
The director of the National Blood Transfusion Center stressed that blood cannot be produced by factories or pharmacies or any high-tech institutes, and that blood can be donated through human donations. Therefore, he called on all Cambodians to donate blood to save human life.
