(Banteay Meanchey): The Teuk Thla Bridge, located in Teuk Thla Village, Sangkat Teuk Thla, Serey Sophorn City, was repainted by the Joint Police Force, Provincial Police, Provincial Gendarmerie, Armed Forces and Banteay Meanchey Provincial Administration on the afternoon of August 24, 2020.
At the same time, there is also clearing of weeds that grow around the bridge and fill in the ground at the end of the bridge to protect, preserve and enhance the beauty of the heritage as a tourist attraction.
Mr. Hil Raya, Governor of Serey Sophorn City, said that the clearing of thorns, weeds, preparation of land to expand the area around the bridge and the painting of rust to protect the Teuk Thla Bridge now under the recommendation of Deputy Prime Minister Ke Kim Yan and Mrs. Mao Malay Ke Kim Yan.
He said that the purpose of this activity is to promote the preservation of steel bridges in the French colonial era and the development of the memorial park, which was established in 2018, on the initiative of Ms. Mao Malay Ke Kim Yan.
On the other hand, to express his gratitude to Samdech Techo Hun Sen, the Prime Minister, for acknowledging Deputy Prime Minister Ke Kim Yan's request to preserve the 84-year-old steel bridge as history and memories. To commemorate the values of peace after the end of the war.
Please be reminded that according to the historical research, it is confirmed that this Teuk Thla Bridge is a steel bridge with UVHL panels, a famous French colonial era, the only original remains along National Road 5 at the head of Stung Serei Sophorn in Teuk Thla Village. Sangkat Teuk Thla, west of Serei Sophorn city, 6 km.
The bridge is 81 meters long, 5 meters wide, has three rows of concrete bridge pillars and was built in 1931 at the same time as the construction of National Road No. 5 Phnom Penh to Poipet with a length of 408 kilometers.
But to this day, the bridge is very dilapidated due to old age and hit by cars, tanks and landmines. The bridge was maintained after the new bridge was built, allowing vehicles to cross, leaving only pedestrians.
When the Prime Minister inaugurated Meanchey University, which is located near the bridge, on April 4, 2008, Samdech Techo agreed to the request of Deputy Prime Minister Ke Kim Yan and the Ministry of Public Works and Transport to maintain the bridge. This means building new roads and stone bridges next to the old steel bridges.
It should also be reminded that recently, this old steel bridge was violated by opportunists who buried the foundation of the bridge to fill its flat land, making the bridge even more unstable. This caused the public to react strongly to the authorities, accusing them of issuing laws allowing land miners without regard to the impact and loss of legacy.
