Here are some of the candidates who are seen as likely to lead Japan in case of Abe's resignation:
Taro Aso, 79, who is currently finance minister and deputy prime minister, is a key member of Abe's administration. In the absence of a clear consensus on who should replace Abe, LDP lawmakers could appoint Aso as interim leader in case Abe resigns.
Former Japanese Defense Minister Shijiro Ishiba, 63, is one of the few voters who would like to see him elected prime minister, but he is less popular among LDP lawmakers. Ishiba once defeated Abe in the first round of party elections in 2012.
Fumino Kishida, 63, served as Japan's foreign minister under Abe's administration from 2012 to 2017. Kishida is seen as a possible successor to Abe. But his popularity is still limited by opinion polls.
Japanese Defense Minister Taro Kono, 56, is known as an independent man, but he adheres to a number of key Abe policies, including a firm stance on the conflict with South Korea. Around wartime history.
Yushidi Shuga, 71, has been loyal to Abe since Abe's tenure as prime minister in 2006 and 2007. Shuga was also one of the people who encouraged Abe to run in the 2012 by-elections.
Katsunu Bukato, the 64-year-old Japanese health minister, was under the watchful eye of the Japanese people during the first outbreak of the COVID-19 virus. Later, 57-year-old Yasutoshi Shinimura, Japan's economy minister, was also closely watched by the Japanese people over the COVID-19 settlement policy.
Sikonoda, 59, has made no secret of her desire to become Japan's first female prime minister. She has been a vocal critic of Abe, but she did not get enough votes in the 2018 LDP leadership race.
