China shares specific information to address water changes in the Mekong River (Video inside) (Phnom Penh): China has announced that it will cooperate and share specific information on water management to address water changes in the Mekong River One of the concerns of countries in the Mekong region today.
The Mekong-Lanchang River covers a total area of 4,880 km, passing through six countries: China, Myanmar, Thailand, Laos, Cambodia and Vietnam. In the Mekong sub-region - Lan Chang.
In 2020, the water level in the Mekong River Basin will change significantly, meaning that the water level in the Mekong River Basin will be lower. This change in water levels has led to serious concerns about the impact on fisheries resources and the scarcity of water for aquaculture in lower Mekong countries such as Cambodia and Vietnam.
Although experts explain that the water changes in the Mekong Basin are caused by climate change, there are some links and blaming that the water changes in the Mekong Basin are caused by the dams in the country. China and Laos.
At the Mekong-Lanchang Summit, which is the cooperation between China and five countries along the Mekong River, including: Laos, Thailand, Myanmar, Cambodia and Vietnam, on August 24, 2020, four countries along the Mekong River were not mentioned. Only China and Vietnam have raised the issue of water changes in the Mekong River.
Chinese Premier Li Keqiang called for more cooperation in the field of water, both in the use and conservation of water with mutual respect. In addition, the countries along the Lancang and Mekong rivers must help each other in common interests and promote cooperation by building consensus.
Li Keqiang affirmed that China is ready to provide assistance to the Mekong countries to use this water resource, and from 2020, China will share water information to the countries along the Mekong River in time during the flood season as well as the rainy season. Drought.
"We will work with you to establish a forum for sharing information on water resources cooperation," he said. "The Lancang-Mekong River is on the Chinese side, so China will play a key role in launching a forum that will be open to all six countries in the mechanism."
"If there is a change in the water level in any situation, if it is difficult for each country to record information about the river, we are ready to provide open data to share and invite experts from other countries," Li Keqiang said. "All to analyze the data and the data will be provided to all relevant countries to use the data as a reference to create an effective response."
The Prime Minister also proposed a regular Mekong-Lanchang Water Resources Ministerial Meeting to address issues.
Vietnam, a country on the lower reaches of the Mekong River, has been the hardest hit by climate change. Vietnamese Prime Minister Nguyen Tan Dung highlighted the difficulties of changing the water level in the Mekong River basin to the inflow of seawater into its Mekong River.
The Vietnamese Prime Minister has come out in full support of the Chinese Prime Minister's will and proposal to address water changes in the Mekong Basin.
"I highly appreciate Prime Minister Li Keqiang's request to share water information throughout the year," said Chinese Premier Nguyen Tan Dung. Sharing water information, Vietnam supports the annual meeting of the Minister of Water Resources to cooperate on environmental protection projects and sustainable management of water resources.
