(Siem Reap): Employer representatives of the Cambodian Cultural Village, Ms. Lim Sopheak, General Manager and Mr. Touch Socheat, Deputy General Manager, on August 23, 2020, came out to confirm the allegations made by the strikers. Employers are reluctant to deal with that is not true.
Representatives of the Cambodian Cultural Village employers confirmed that the settlement with more than 40 workers has been negotiated, but no agreement has been reached. At the same time, the employers of the Cambodian Cultural Village still show their position to participate in resolving this labor dispute as soon as possible.
Employers of the Cambodian Cultural Village explained that during the onset of the Kovid-19 epidemic in Cambodia, as well as around the world, Siem Reap's tourism sector was severely affected: restaurants, hotels, resorts and homes. The lodge is closed.
Cambodia Cultural Village Resort also suffered a severe financial crisis due to the decline in its income due to the decline of national and international tourists, but to avoid the complete closure of the business and to keep the work of workers remain the same Cultural Village Solve the most difficult problem for more than 300 workers.
The management team of Cambodian Cultural Village Company has tried its best to create unique and beautiful landscapes, innovations to attract national and international tourists to visit Cambodian Cultural Village, which is different from previous years.
In addition, the Cambodian Cultural Village Company is a supportive part of the national culture and part of the national economy, which helps local people to have jobs, improve their livelihoods and avoid migration.
The company has tried to solve the problem of better pay than suspension (no pay to workers), the company has met with the supervisor and all workers about the payment of 80% and reduce working hours 20 % Equal to 6 and a half hours per day for workers with a salary below 200 US dollars, the company has no deductions and reduced working hours by 20%.
More than 200 workers agreed to accept because they were aware of the company's deteriorating situation, but more than 40 strikers did not agree. The Arbitration Council's order on the collective agreement was set for September 9, 2020, but the strikers still did not agree.
On August 17, 2020, HE Nak Neron, Deputy Governor of Siem Reap Province, chaired a bilateral meeting to mediate and suspend the strike from this Khmer New Year holiday. However, on August 22, 2020, the workers resumed their strike, arguing that there was no solution to the strike.
According to the leadership of the Department of Labor and Vocational Training, the Siem Reap Provincial Administration and the specialized departments, as well as the relevant institutions and units, have continued the process of conciliation to find a solution. Iran reveals data on black box of first Ukrainian plane shot down after being sent to France for analysis
Tehran: The Iranian Civil Aviation Organization on Sunday said that an analysis of the black box of a Ukrainian plane that its Revolutionary Guards mistakenly shot down in January showed that it had been attacked. Two missiles while all the passengers on board were still alive after being hit by the first bullet. This is according to the release by CNA on Sunday, August 23, 2020.
In a statement issued on Sunday, the Iranian Civil Aviation Organization said: "19 seconds after the first missile struck the pilot's voice plane in the cabin, it shows that all passengers are still alive. "... 25 seconds after the second missile struck the plane."
The Iranian civil aviation announcement is the first official report on the contents of the audio recordings and data recorded in the flight from the black box, which was sent to France for analysis in July last year. 2020 ago.
Iran has admitted that its Revolutionary Guards mistakenly shot down a Ukrainian International Airlines flight PS752 on January 8, 2020, as tensions between Iran and the United States reached high. The point of imminent large-scale war. The crash killed a total of 175 people, 55 of them Canadians.
