One of the tourists described that if the Preah Vihear provincial authorities do not take measures to build a detour to climb the Tbeng mountain in time, they will not be able to visit this long holiday. Recreation on the slopes of Tbeng Mountain is not successful because some of the stairs are old, dilapidated and runny. Therefore, they would like to thank Mr. Prak Sovann, Governor of Preah Vihear Province as well.
It should be noted that 59 stairs up to Tbeng Mountain, equal to 31 meters, collapsed on August 19, 2020, but did not cause any casualties. The collapse was caused by a dilapidated 163-meter-long staircase, while the bottom and top of the ladder were usable, according to Preah Vihear provincial governor Prak Sovann.
The provincial governor said that after leading a team to inspect the dilapidated stairs on August 6, 2020, he assigned the provincial Department of Public Works and Transport to study the repair project and the provincial police commissioner to deploy. 24 hours to check, no more than 5 people are allowed to go up and down at a time, and the team to pave the way between the stairs more than 100 meters.
As nearly 5,000 national and international tourists climbed the mountain on August 17, the team reported to him for comment, and he decided to stop traffic on the dilapidated stairs, allowing them to walk up and down. Bypass, on August 18, 2020, suddenly the next day, the collapse occurred.
