Japanese Foreign Minister arrives in Cambodia
(Phnom Penh): Japanese Foreign Minister Motigito Shimizu arrived in Cambodia at 5:30 pm on August 21, 2020 on a special plane of the Government of Japan to pay his first official visit. In Cambodia as Minister of Foreign Affairs from today until August 23.
According to the program planned during the visit, the Japanese Foreign Minister will pay a courtesy call on Cambodian Prime Minister Samdech Techo Hun Sen on the morning of August 22. In the afternoon, Motigito Shimitsu will meet with Deputy Prime Minister Prak Sokhon, Minister of Foreign Affairs and International Cooperation.
A number of bilateral and multilateral issues of common interest are expected to be discussed at the meeting.
Due to the outbreak of Kovid 19 until August 2020, Cambodia has not yet received senior officials from any friendly countries. The visit of Motigito Shimitsu can be considered as the first visit by a high-ranking official of a friendly country who visited Cambodia during the 19th Kovid crisis. This visit is also considered as a special and important visit.
Prior to his arrival, Moto Gito Simitsu also sent a message to Cambodians. In this message, Mr. Motigito Shimitsu highlighted the progress and development of the good relations between Cambodia and Japan.
Mr. Motigito Simitsu has been to Cambodia many times, but in different positions and roles. In 2014, as Minister of Economy, Trade and Industry, Motigito Simitsu visited Cambodia and brought with him the executives of several Japanese companies to promote Cambodia's economic transformation.
The Japanese diplomat also stressed that he looks forward to meeting with Cambodian Prime Minister Samdech Techo Hun Sen and Deputy Prime Minister Prak Sokhon to continue the two countries' strategic partnership. Grow more.
(Phnom Penh): Longvek City was first built in 1525-1527 AD, 2068-2070 under the hands of King Chan Reach, located in Longvek Commune, Kampong Tralach District, Kampong Chhnang Province, but now the strong capital of the Khmer people. This Angkorian period suffered two invasions by the Siamese army and was completely dismantled in 1594 AD (2137).
In connection with the above historical event, many people wondered who the Siamese king had the power to break this strong city of Longvek?
After the death of King Chan Reachea in 1566 (2109 CE), his eldest son, King Boramin Raja or King Boram Reach, succeeded Longvek. His Majesty King Bhumibol Adulyadej was a very brave king who continued his royal duties to reclaim western Khmer territory from Siam from the time he ascended the throne until his death.
After the death of King Borin Nang of Burma in 1581 (AD 2124), the Kingdom of Siam gradually emerged from the Burmese rule. The Siamese king who led the uprising was Prince Noraso (before he became king). This Nore So, who later led the Siamese army to invade Longvek of the Khmer Kingdom.
But less than a year later, the kingdom of Ayutthaya was defeated by the Burmese army in August 1569 (AD 2112). When the conquest of Srey Ayutthaya was achieved, the Burmese king Borin Nang forced King Mahindra Thiraj to transfer the throne to his successor, Mahadhamma Rajathiraj, to reign in Srey Ayutthaya instead.
King Borin Nang then ordered King Mahinda to lose the war and go to Hangsavatey to prevent future disasters. During his stay in Hangsavat, King Mahinda was suffering from severe pain due to the Burmese aggression against the Siamese people at that time, until 1569 (AD 2112). Died in Hangsavatey at the age of 30.
In 1574 (AD 2117), the Burmese army of King Borin Nang attacked from the west, while the Khmer army of King Bhumibol Adulyadej attacked from the east, forcing the Siamese king, King Mahadhamma, to sign a treaty with His Majesty the King returned some provinces to the Khmer, including Nakhon Ratchasima, Chan Borei, and Western Borei.
In 1576 (AD 2119), a brave Khmer king died in Longvek, and his eldest son, King Sattha I, succeeded his father in Longvek. Second, Princess Srey Suryaporn is the Grand Viceroy.
While the Siamese were at war with Burma, King Sattha I issued a royal order ordering the viceroy Srey Suryaporn to raise troops to help the Siamese expel the Burmese army in accordance with the Khmer-Siamese treaty signed during the reign of King Bhumibol Adulyadej. Until the Siamese were able to reclaim their control from Burma and declared Siam independence from May 1584 (2127).
In December 1595 (AD 2138), King Norseo and Prince Ekatasrat raised troops to invade Hangsavati again, but could not defeat Hangsavatey, so he withdrew. Srey Ayutthaya.
