Mr. Moto Gito Simitsu will arrive in Cambodia on the evening of August 21, 2020 for a three-day visit to Cambodia from August 21 to 23. Prior to his arrival through the Embassy of Japan in Cambodia, Motigito Simitsu sent a message to Cambodians.
In this regard, Mr. Motigito Shimitsu highlighted the relationship and cooperation between Japan and Cambodia. The Japanese diplomat pointed out that in 2014, as the Minister of Economy, Trade and Industry, he visited Cambodia and brought with the executives of many Japanese companies to promote economic transformation in Cambodia.
The Japanese diplomat emphasized that after the civil war in Cambodia, Japan has joined in supporting the peace process, reconstruction, as well as the continuous development of Cambodia, which is the foundation of the friendship between the two countries. Second, the relationship is deepened in areas such as politics, security, economy, culture and human resource exchange.
He said that the number of Cambodians living in Japan increased 4.3 times (from 3,436 to 15,020). Cambodia is at the heart of the Southern Economic Corridor, which connects the Mekong region, and half of the population is young and energetic under the age of 25, which has the potential to deepen bilateral ties.
He also reminded that in January 2020, he met with 10 young Cambodians with political interests who visited Japan, and all 10 young Cambodians expressed their desire to develop Cambodia. Strong.
Motigito Shimitsu praised Cambodia's human resources, comparing Cambodia's human resources to the tops of the Angkor Wat and Angkor Thom temples, which were supported by solid stones stacked on top of each other. On the other hand, just like the Angkorian era, which flourished by coexistence and mixing of Hinduism and Buddhism, Cambodia has a history and tradition of embracing different cultures, ideas and human resources.
After describing the achievements of these collaborations, Mr. Motigito Shimitsu stressed that Japan will continue to support and assist Cambodia in achieving a development path linked to the youth.
"Japan will continue to provide support so that Cambodia can move forward on its path to democratic development by incorporating youth strength as a pillar," said Motigito Shimitsu.
The Japanese diplomat also stated that he looks forward to meeting with Cambodian Prime Minister Samdech Techo Hun Sen and Deputy Prime Minister Prak Sokhon to further the two countries' strategic partnership. Grow more.
Motigito Shimitsu will arrive in Cambodia on the evening of August 21, 2020 for his first official visit to Cambodia as Minister of Foreign Affairs of Japan for three days, from the 21st to the 21st. August 23 During the visit, the Japanese diplomat will pay a courtesy call and discuss with Samdech Techo Hun Sen on the morning of August 22.
In the afternoon of the same day, the Minister will meet with Deputy Prime Minister Prak Sokhon, Minister of Foreign Affairs and International Cooperation. In this meeting, there will be discussions on some bilateral and multilateral issues of common interest.
It should be noted that under the lively relations between Cambodia and the friendly countries, each year, Cambodia receives the leaders of the friendly countries no less. But for 2020, due to the crisis of Kovid 19 until August, no leaders of friendly countries have visited Cambodia yet.
Mote Gito Simitsu can be considered as the first senior official of a friendly country to visit Cambodia in 2020 during the crisis caused by Kovid 19 disease.
Please note that Cambodia and Japan were officially connected on January 9, 1953. Cambodia-Japan relations continued to improve until becoming a strategic partnership in December 2013.
Japanese investment projects are located in Phnom Penh Special Economic Zone, Manhattan Bavet, Tai Seng, Bavet Dragon, We Bavet Port, Sihanoukville, Sanco, Poipet and Kok Koh Kong.
Japan is also one of the leading donors to the Khmer Rouge tribunal, providing a total of $ 85.12 million from 2005 to 2016), equivalent to 32% of international financial assistance. Of that, $ 16.54 million for the national side and $ 68.58 million for the international side. In 2017, Japan provided an additional $ 1.2 million for the international side of the Khmer Rouge tribunal.
In addition to assistance to the Khmer Rouge tribunal, Japan has provided millions of dollars in development assistance to Cambodia in the areas of education, infrastructure improvement, humanitarian work, and other social issues.
(Cambodia): There have long been questions regarding the "behavior of the original statue of Jayavarman VII", with some researchers having found that he had a meditative gesture of Kong Pen, Preah Phen, and both hands above the eyes. However, there is no clear evidence of this claim yet, only a while later, experts found the truth.
At the 33rd Technical Meeting of the ICC-Angkor Committee on December 10, 2019, a joint working group from the Apsara Authority and the French School of the Far East revealed the true nature of the King's statue. Jayavarman VII is in "the act of raising the hand to the chest."
To this end, the Joint Working Group presented a wealth of evidence relating to the study of history, archeology, scans, scientific analysis of the size and assembly of artifacts kept at the Angkor Preserve to the body of the statue. Jayavarman VII really matched all the angles at the connecting points.
