A wall of a hospital building in Banteay Meanchey province collapsed, causing a girl to die tragically
(Banteay Meanchey): The wall of the Cambodia-Japan Friendship Hospital in Mongkol Borei collapsed, causing a young girl to die suddenly, while the authorities confirmed that it was not the fault of any unit or person at this hospital .
Prior to the incident, he decided to take care of his mother at the Cambodia-Japan Friendship Hospital in Mongkol Borei. One year, a 6-year-old boy named Samrech Sieu Er was playing near the end of the hammock when a 1.2-meter-long and 1-meter-wide stone wall collapsed on him, killing him.
At the same time, the medical staff advised and forbade the patient and the attendant to cling or sit or cradle on the railing of the building for fear of further danger, but the patient and the family criticized that in this case, the hospital unit should write a warning sign and forbid Touch the toilet or repair it first to avoid such an accident.
However, after the accident, the doctors were diligent in rescuing the child victim, but the situation was too serious to help, the hospital sent a representative to attend the funeral and 1 million Riel.
The director of the Banteay Meanchey Provincial Health Department, Dr. Le Chansangvat, said that the department did not immediately receive a report of the case and only now knew that the judicial authorities had closed the case.
Mongkul Borei District Police and Banteay Meanchey Provincial Police said that the case of breaking the handrail of the balcony wall of the Cambodia-Japan Friendship Hospital in Mongkol Borei, causing the death of the child, was investigated in detail and the result was an accident. It is not caused by humans, so the authorities closed the case.
(Cambodia): Khmer ancestors of the Angkorian period built numerous stone temples throughout the ancient Khmer Empire, especially in the present Angkor area, which was the capital of Cambodia at that time, while the construction of each temple cost sandstone. Lots and lots.
In the presence of many Khmer kings, the new capital provided us with a number of ancient temples, such as Phnom Bakheng, Baphuon, Takeo, Banteay Srei, Angkor Wat, Bayon, Angkor Thom, and Chea. Much more.
O Thmor Dab is located in Beng Mealea commune, Svay Leu district, Siem Reap province, now more than 50 kilometers from Angkor, between the foothills of Kulen Mountain, southeast and northwest of Beng Mealea Temple. The area is lined with huge boulders stretching under small bushes.
Mr. Phon Dara continued that the means of transporting stones from the O Thmor Dap area to build temples in the Angkor area is that the Khmer ancestors used waterways rather than land. Once excavated from the rocky area, they floated the raft along the Sramoch canal down to the Tonle Sap Lake and from the Tonle Sap Lake up the Siem Reap River to the present-day Angkor site. Although this waterway takes a long time, it has reduced the transport capacity more than by land.
