Dr. Ouk Maly installs lamps under the three pillars of the Temple of the Trinity, Cambodian Buddhist Cultural Center
(Kampong Speu): Dr. Ouk Maly, Deputy Governor of the National Bank of Cambodia, and his family attended the Cremation Ceremony for the Trinity, or simply the installation of light bulbs at the bottom of the three shrines to illuminate the Buddha. The Trinity in the Great Temple holds the Trinity of the Cambodian Buddhist Cultural Center, located in Kirirom National Park on the territory of Kampong Speu Province.
The ceremony took place on the afternoon of August 16, 2020, in accordance with the Buddhist tradition, with the invitation of monks to recite the Samathasil according to the seminar to commemorate the high Preah Ratanatray in the glorious Buddhist tradition. In particular, we commemorate our Master Samma Samputra, who was enlightened by the sublime Samma Sampothinya and the great Dharma Discipline left by Buddhists of all generations to this day.
His teachings and Dharma are the beacon for all human beings to adhere to good deeds, meritorious deeds, renounce sins, and build meritorious deeds for the future of the nation.
Afterwards, Rev. Dr. Ouk Mali and her family, as well as monks and Buddhists, led by Venerable Nhek Buntha, marched on the Lantern to the Great Temple to pay homage to the Trinity and light a traditional Buddhist ceremony. This is under the pedestal of the three peaks of the Great Temple to illuminate the Trinity, which is inscribed on a stone tablet kept in this huge temple, which is about 90% under construction.
According to Dr. Nhek Buntha, the center has started construction on a 126-hectare land concession from the Royal Government located in Kirirom National Park, Kampong Speu province, with a budget of tens of millions of dollars. Considered to be the largest Buddhist cultural center ever in the history of Cambodia and will become one of the largest national cultural treasures in Cambodia.
The construction of these buildings to promote Buddhism, especially to the merits of ordinary people and as the property of our nation.
According to the plan, the Cambodian Buddhist Cultural Center will build four monasteries and a shrine in the middle of the four monasteries. % That is planned to spend about 3.5 million dollars.
At present, the center is continuing to build the Chulamuni Stupa to house 84,000 Buddha statues, which is 300 square meters in size and has 29 nine-story galleries in the style of Angkor temples.
As of now, the construction of both the first temple and the achievements in the Cambodian Buddhist Cultural Center has cost more than 5 million dollars.
With this, the great devotee also thanked the King, the Prince of Life on the Stone, the King of the Kingdom of Cambodia, and the Queen Mother of the Kingdom of Cambodia, both of whom donated their royal property. The process of building this center up.
In addition, the Archbishop would like to thank Samdech Techo Hun Sen, Prime Minister of Cambodia, and Samdech Kittipritthbandit Bun Rany Hun Sen, as well as Samdech's mother, Duke Prith Moha Upavathana Bun Seangly, for donating funds for the construction. This magnificent Buddhist structure has long been considered a national treasure.
With this, the devotee also thanked Mrs. Ouk Mali and her family, as well as the relatives of Preah Dharma, who have jointly purchased a light bulb to be installed in the Great Temple to hold the Trinity now and to receive merits. Everyone. Do not forget to visit Siem Reap! "Relax Siem Reap" held a prayer ceremony at Angkor Wat for happiness
