Hing Soksan leaks Sam Rainsy story, plans to get rid of Kem Sokha in 2014
(Phnom Penh): Political events in Cambodia after the 5th legislative election in 2013, perhaps the Cambodian people as voters have not forgotten. The result of the election was that the CPP won 68 seats and the CNRP from the Sam Rainsy Party's Sam Rainsy Party and Kem Sokha's Human Rights Party won 55 seats.
This election result made the CNRP refuse to accept the election results, refuse to enter the National Assembly and take the road as a hostage of the demonstration, demanding to review the election results and finally demanding the removal of Samdech Techo Hun Sen from the post of Prime Minister. .
It was not until 2014 that there was political coordination and negotiation between the CPP and the CNRP, with some concessions in the National Assembly, and the CNRP agreed to sit in the National Assembly building.
The negotiation process at that time was known as the opening of negotiations between Samdech Techo Hun Sen and Mr. Sam Rainsy until there was a family meal of the two party leaders.
At that time, there were many questions and surprises about the decision of the CNRP to agree to work in the National Assembly, because they initially expressed a firm position not to work in the National Assembly with the CPP. Call it the "Thief" Parliament.
Hing Soksan, the then youth leader of the Cambodia National Rescue Party (CNRP) at the time, recounted some of the secrets of the process of agreeing to enter parliament.
Mr. Hing Soksan revealed that after secret talks between Samdech Techo Hun Sen and Mr. Sam Rainsy, Mr. Sam Rainsy decided to enter the National Assembly and Mr. Kem Sokha did not know anything. Mr. Hing Soksan recalled that at that time Mr. Kem Sokha and he were on a working visit to India were forced by Mr. Sam Rainsy to return home to discuss the work to enter the National Assembly.
To make matters worse, after Kem Sokha continued to refuse to enter the parliament according to Sam Rainsy's wishes, they also devised a tactic to get rid of Kem Sokha. That event took place in July 2014, when Mr. Sam Rainsy's group staged a demonstration scenario to shift the responsibility to Mr. Kem Sokha.
Mr. Hing Soksan reminded that in July 2014, Mr. Sam Rainsy, the party's president, went abroad, so the responsibility of the party falls on Mr. Kem Sokha as vice president. At that time, Ms. Mu Sochua repeatedly insisted on Mr. Kem Sokha to protest, but Mr. Kem Sokha always refused.
Then, on July 15, 2014, Ms. Mu Sochua and some other lawmakers, who are Mr. Sam Rainsy's quota, demonstrated and led to a violent explosion, injuring several Daun Penh district security guards. Following the outbreak of violence, Mu Sochua and other CNRP lawmakers, as well as some pro-CNRP youths, were arrested.
Mr. Hing Soksan concluded that at that time, if Mr. Kem Sokha had any agreement for Ms. Mu Sochua to demonstrate, the authorities would have arrested Mr. Kem Sokha, but the telephone conversation was known and no arrests were made.
According to Mr. Hing Soksan, the plan to push Mr. Kem Sokha to prison is not to be a backbone to the decision to enter the National Assembly of Mr. Sam Rainsy at that time. Police arrest 4 more people for cheating as employees of sending and withdrawing money, cheating money from sending and withdrawing agents
