According to Mr. Phay Siphan, the "National Cassava Policy 2020-2025" was established with the aim of making Cambodia a place for production, processing and a sustainable supplier of cassava products for the regional and global markets.
The Deputy Prime Minister affirmed that through the National Cassava Policy 2020-2025, Cambodia expects to make Cambodian cassava products a strategic crop that provides more income to farmers and is the basis for the development of the processing industry in Cambodia. Cambodia. In addition, Cambodia can really step towards the development of the national economy by using cassava products to promote growth and share the benefits of this growth to all stakeholders.
More than 90,000 rural families in 13 provinces are growing cassava and many workers are working in various fields such as planting, harvesting, processing and exporting, the report said. There are 550 cassava collection points, mostly in five provinces: Battambang, Pailin, Banteay Meanchey, Oddar Meanchey and Tbong Khmum.
The report also points out that China is the largest buyer in the world and Thailand and Vietnam are the largest suppliers to the international market, while Cambodia acts as a cassava grower.
The report concludes that Cambodia's neighboring countries could not expand their cassava cultivation areas because their agricultural areas were clearly defined and subdivided.
The report said that the Asian cassava market has established a value chain in the region where Cambodia is the primary grower, harvester and processor, while neighboring countries that control the market and have a competitive advantage in trade play a leading role. Out and negotiate prices.
Cassava is an agro-commercial crop for export and is not a staple food for the Cambodian people, but it is an important staple food of many Indonesians, Indians, Africans and Latin Americans.
