Both his grandfather and father were former generals of the Gujarat state, which shows that Mahatma Gandhi's status was not poor and difficult. In 1869, at the age of 13, Gandhi married because of a local custom that required people to marry at an early age.
He went on to graduate from Ahmedabad University and decided to study law in London, England. Gandhi studied in London in 1886 and studied for three years before returning to India and setting up a law firm in Mumbai. He tried for two years, but to no avail, and returned to South Africa.
The reason he decided to go back was because there are a lot of Indians living in South Africa and there is hope for a better career as a lawyer in India. Life in South Africa is the time to start becoming a politician again because of one of the most disgusting and shameful cases of discrimination.
* About a lawyer becoming a politician:
The whites complained to the security guards and ordered Gandhi to be evicted from the first floor. The security guards came and told him to move to the third floor, which was uncomfortable, and the price was very cheap, even though Kanthy had bought the right ticket for the first floor. Gandhi denied this, exacerbating the conflict on the first floor of the train.
Shortly after arriving at the first station, Gandhi was thrown from the train, much to his shame, and the pain made him want to change and break free from the yoke of British colonial rule. A week later he convened an Indian immigration meeting and his ideas grew beyond expectations.
It should be noted that the story of Gandhi in South Africa is the most bitter story in life, being kicked, beaten, thrown, beaten, insulted, despised, despised in every way, but still able to build. Life in South Africa marked the beginning of a new life for Gandhi, which made Gandhi an Indian shrine and the reason for the nickname "Mahatma Gandhi" since then.
* About the reasons for the withdrawal from the UK:
At that time in South Africa, Indians and Africans were always under white capitalism. Mahatma Gandhi began to expand the community from more ethnic and religious backgrounds and make them live together equally.
In 1906, a new law required all nationalities to register and be thumbprinted, even ordering Indian women to take off their clothes in front of white authorities. In a fit of rage, more than 3,000 Indians gathered in a public place to protest the law.
In 1913, white supremacist authorities in South Africa issued another law declaring Hindu and Islamic marriages null and void. This made Kanthy create more protests throughout the country. Under the new marriage law, more than 50,000 workers have stopped working, prompting white supremacy to repeal the law. This is why Kanthy believes that the masses can overcome oppression.
* Action on independence from Britain:
In 1915, Mahatma Gandhi returned to India, where he was 45 years old. Back in his homeland, Mahatma Gandhi began to see the hardships of the Indians, who lived under the strict rule and oppression of their people, leaving them in poverty. At that time, he had the idea that his people really wanted ownership of the land, that is, they did not need the rule of other countries.
In 1919, he inspired people across the country to stand up against white supremacy. Subsequently, inspirations against British rule were also very active. Mahatma Gandhi taught the people to be tolerant and tolerant, but not to give up on demanding freedom.
Nevertheless, any rallies to protest were subjected to severe repression by the British authorities. Not only that, Mahatma Gandhi was often arrested and imprisoned many times by the British authorities.
Despite the crackdown, the opposition movement led by Mahatma Gandhi did not give up, with more and more people participating. It is noteworthy that when he was imprisoned, Mahatma Gandhi worked against the British without eating or drinking anything.
