The call to mobilize Australian investors to do business in Cambodia in the wake of the calm Kovid-19 epidemic was made this morning, August 13, 2020, on the occasion that he allowed Mr. Pablo Kang The Australian Ambassador paid a courtesy call and discussed a number of issues at the Council of Ministers.
Speaking on the occasion, Deputy Prime Minister Hor Namhong mentioned that Cambodia-Australia diplomatic relations had been connected since 1952, before Cambodia gained independence from France. Since then, Australia has provided assistance to Cambodia every year, as well as providing scholarships for Cambodian students and officials to study and do internships in Australia.
Meanwhile, the Royal Government of Cambodia has provided more than US $ 125 million in assistance to the poor affected by Kovid-19, as well as support to hundreds of thousands of workers who are losing their jobs during Kovid-19, $ 40 per month.
The Deputy Prime Minister said that in the face of the global Kovid-19 epidemic, the international community should work together to eradicate this deadly disease. The discovery of the Kovid-19 vaccine must be for global health, not politically or commercially.
Taking this opportunity, the Deputy Prime Minister also thanked the Australian Government for facilitating the integration of the Cambodian people in Australia with a dignified life in the Australian community.
Regarding the Cambodia-Australia economic relationship, the former Cambodian ambassador said that Cambodia-Australia trade is still at an inappropriate level and the potential of the two countries in 2019 is only 249 million dollars, which the Deputy Prime Minister requested the balance of trade between the two countries. Two to be more active.
Regarding ASEAN, Ambassador Pablo Kang called for a balance between the superpowers and that Australia remains supportive of ASEAN's core role and on the issue of the South China Sea, in which China claims much of its regional sovereignty.
At the same time, Deputy Prime Minister Hor Namhong praised Australia for supporting ASEAN's core role in regional design and insisted on respecting ASEAN's core role to facilitate peace, security and cooperation in the region. In particular, the issue of the South China Sea is a matter of sovereignty between some ASEAN member countries and China should be resolved through bilateral negotiations.
