In order to be successful in his work, the Minister instructed all officials to maintain unity, love, understanding and close cooperation in the work, because unity is a factor that leads to success in work, as a Khmer proverb says, "A chopstick The seizures do not break. ” Ministry of Agriculture presents measures to respond to the effects of climate change on agricultural production (Video inside)
(Phnom Penh): The Ministry of Agriculture, Forestry and Fisheries, through a press conference organized by the Royal Government Spokesperson Unit this morning, August 13, 2020, presented what are the measures to respond to climate change on agricultural production.
The Ministry of Agriculture, Forestry and Fisheries has a long-term vision for 2030: Cambodian agriculture is a modern, competitive, climate-resilient and sustainable environment that leads to increased household incomes, prosperity and well-being of the Cambodian people.
To achieve this vision, four strategic goals are implemented:
* Strategic Goal 1: Enhancing the competitive agricultural production chain * Strategic Goal 2: Increasing support for agricultural infrastructure and facilitating agricultural trade * Strategic Goal 3: Enhancing sustainable land, forest and fisheries management * Strategic Goal 4: Strengthen institutions, legal norms and human resource development.
The Ministry of Agriculture, Forestry and Fisheries has taken measures to respond to climate change, including:
* First, to increase the capacity of institutions to develop policies, strategic action plans by setting up technical working groups to develop policies and strategies in response to the effects of climate change, strategic action plans to respond to the effects of climate change, and technical guidelines on implementation. Climate-wise agriculture and plans to develop policies and strategies on agricultural crop insurance.
* Second: Build the capacity of technical officers, officers and agricultural extension agents and farmers from the understanding and implementation and climate-wise agricultural technology. Climate-wise agriculture practices aim to increase productivity and income, increase climate resilience and adaptation, and reduce greenhouse gas emissions.
* Third: Promote the research and dissemination of new techniques and the application of climate-resilient techniques: short-lived varieties, modern techniques (in planting and crop management), mixed crop care measures, mixed soil management measures and agricultural soil fertility. Preserve modern and water-saving irrigation technology and house systems, nets and post-harvest technology, etc.
* Fourth: Promote the rehabilitation and construction of climate-resilient physical infrastructure for agriculture with irrigation system by diverting into rice fields, modern irrigation systems, warehouses, dryers, roads connecting rice fields, agricultural communities to markets and the application of modern technology, etc.
* 5th: Promote the use of renewable energy, solar energy, biogas plant and compost, etc.
* Eighth: Global cooperation in the region and partners to implement programs and projects such as climate-resilient new agricultural programs, environmentally friendly agricultural production projects, the Agricultural Production Value Chain (CAVAC) program and research on resilient technologies. Climate change, such as resistant cultivars, intelligent farming and climate (CSA), the use of satellite systems as a basis for supporting rice crop insurance.
According to Mak Soeun, Deputy Director General of the Ministry of Agriculture, Cambodia is vulnerable to climate change, with about 80 percent of the population dependent on agriculture, especially in the central lowlands and the Tonle Sap (Mekong Basin). Rainfed rice fields cover more than 2.5 million hectares, climate change awareness is limited and technical capacity, budget and institutions are still limited in terms of climate change response measures.
(Phnom Penh): On the morning of August 13, 2020, the Royal Government Spokesperson Unit held a press conference on "Impacts and Measures to Respond to Climate Change in Agricultural Production" with the participation of speakers from the Ministry of Agriculture. Forestry and fishing.
The purpose of this conference is to present to the public a clear understanding of the impact and effective response measures of the Ministry of Agriculture, Forestry and Fisheries to meet the challenges of the agricultural sector, especially the crop sub-sector in the circumstances. Of Climate Change Situations.
According to a report by the Ministry of Agriculture, Cambodia is vulnerable to climate change because about 80 percent of the population depends on agriculture, especially in the central lowlands and the Tonle Sap (Mekong Basin), rainfed rice fields. With more than 2.5 million hectares, climate change awareness is limited and technical capacity, resources, budget and institutions are still limited in terms of climate change response measures.
The Minister of Health requested the Australian Ambassador to cooperate in facilitating diplomats, officials, international organizations, and businessmen who come to Cambodia to strictly implement health measures.
(Phnom Penh): The Minister of Health, Prof. Mam Bunheng, requested the Australian Ambassador to Cambodia, Pablo Kang, to co-operate in facilitating the arrival of diplomats, officials of international organizations and Australian businessmen who are coming to Cambodia to implement the measures. Health.
In particular, the health measures that the Royal Government of Cambodia issued on August 5, 2020, which will be implemented from August 17, 2020, along with the notification on the adjustment of travel conditions for foreigners. Hold Cambodia, released on August 4, 2020.
This is according to the press release of the Ministry of Health from the meeting between the Minister of Health and the Australian Ambassador to Cambodia on August 13, 2020, which took place at the Ministry.
The meeting also highlighted the main objectives, including:
* First, discuss Australia's cooperation and support in responding to Kovid and economic recovery after the Kovid-19 crisis.
* Third: Discuss the preparation of health measures for diplomats and international organizations holding diplomatic A and B official visas.
On that occasion, the Minister of Health extended a warm welcome to the Australian Ambassador and thanked the Australian Government and people for their support of the health sector since the 1993 general election and are currently assisting the H-EQIP project. First, to continue to contribute with other development partners to prepare the second step in response to the priority strategic plan of the Ministry of Health.
The Minister also recalled the cooperation in the field of health between the two countries, especially from 2007 to the present, the Australian Government has provided health cooperation to Cambodia in the amount of A $ 145 million in the bilateral framework. And will continue to provide an additional A $ 28.9 million over the next five years until 2021, for a total of A $ 173.9 million on various projects.
At the same time, Minister Mam Bunheng expressed his deep gratitude for the support of the Royal Government and the people of Australia in the spirit of solidarity, friendship and cooperation between the two countries, especially in the current circumstances that we have been working together. To promote measures to prevent and respond to the spread of Kovid-19 as soon as possible.
