The bilateral free trade agreement is aimed at deepening trade, investment, services and cooperation and aims to achieve a total trade target of 10 billion US dollars by 2023.
According to Sok Sopheak, Secretary of State of the Ministry of Commerce, after the entry into force of the Cambodia-China Free Trade Agreement, Cambodia's exports to China will increase by about 25 percent. As of 2019, Cambodia's exports to China have reached $ 1 billion, and imports from China have reached nearly $ 7 billion.
Cambodia's free trade with China was seen by Dr. Ky Sereywat, President of the Chinese Studies Center of the Royal Academy of Cambodia, as another venture of the Royal Government of Cambodia to establish a new trade pole outside Europe and the United States. . Which can make Cambodia escape the economic pressure of the Western powers as well.
The Cambodia-China Free Trade Agreement was also hailed by Dr. Sok Touch, President of the Royal Academy of Cambodia and a researcher on trade issues between Cambodia and China, for the achievement of a new trade policy. Karma with the Chinese. But he worries that if Cambodia is not ready, it will slow down production and lead to little benefit from free trade.
In addition to encouraging cooperation between relevant ministries and institutions, Dr. Sok Touch also raised a number of challenges that make it difficult for Cambodia to take advantage of free trade. Challenges include physical infrastructure, production facilities and higher transport costs than neighboring countries, a lack of human resources in heavy and medium industries and a lack of modern technology.
Dr. Sok Touch stated: "Do not worry, they are not afraid to invest in Cambodia, but Cambodia has three important roles: first, transportation, second, the office must be cleared, and third, professionalism, if not all of this is "No, and another point is electricity."
In addition, the President of the Royal Academy also recommended extra caution for goods from neighboring countries, labeled in Cambodia and exported to China. This will lead to serious losses for Cambodia.
