The physicist immigrated to the United States in 1933 and became a lecturer at Princeton University until his retirement in 1945.
Anh Sthanh's daily routine is not strange, it is around 9 to 10 o'clock in the morning, he eats breakfast and check the documents to be prepared for daily teaching very carefully. At about 10:30 in the morning, he went to teach at the university, while the university train always picked him up.
Anh Sthanh works until 1pm and returns home at 1:30 pm for lunch. Then he slept for a while and ate only one cup. His afternoon breaks are like staying home, doing a little work, receiving guests and looking after some of the work that his assistant arranges. Anh Sthanh had dinner at 6:30 and started working again.
He lives a simple life, but he is still a well-known figure at Princeton University. Please be informed that Anh Sthanh's fame is not only due to his scientific excellence, but also because of his erratic attitude and organization.
Walking back and forth from work is always watched by people living in the area because they really want to meet the father of physics like him. One of his co-workers remembers that Anh Sthanh always stood or sat and chatted with his wife, children or grandchildren who lived in the area because they wanted to get funny words and good ideas from him.
Although Anh Sthan is known around the world as the father of nuclear weapons, there are some opinions that he is not the father of nuclear weapons for two reasons:
1. In the theory of nuclear weapons, one of the most important theories is nuclear treatment, and the most famous scientist to discover this theory was Leo Svilard.
2. Anh Sthanh did not participate in the production of US nuclear weapons during the study period by scientists and the Manhattan project. He was also denied access to the Manhattan plot by the US military because he was blacklisted by the FBI as an anti-US German.
"He did not know what weapons were used in World War III, but if there was a fourth world war, people would fight with sticks," he said. And only stones. ”
