(Svay Rieng): Officers of Consumer Protection, Competition and Anti-Fraud Branch in Svay Rieng Province (KP) this morning, August 12, 2020, inspected goods at Kro Ko Market, Svay Chrum District, tested 36 samples of foodstuffs. Found in the bubble contains a total of 11 kilograms of chemicals and some pastries expired and confiscated.
Mr. Pheng Nil, Head of Consumer Protection, Competition and Anti-Fraud Branch in Svay Rieng Province, said that today's inspection was aimed at finding chemicals that traders sell on vegetables, fish, meat and various food items. In particular, packaging beyond the expiration date.
He said that after a preliminary test on food, experts found chemicals on 11 kilograms of soybeans and found some food items out of date. Officials have made a contract to confiscate the people or traders to resell, as well as a contract not to have a second time.
He said that chemicals and food that are out of date to use and affect the health of consumers, especially inflammation of the stomach, intestines and kidneys, causing the body to weaken and lose life. Specialists will inspect foodstuffs throughout the province twice a month to find out chemicals and expired food and packaging.
Mr. Pheng Nil also advertised and appealed to traders, people, both sellers and buyers, before buying something to eat, please check the date of use clearly.
Residents around the barn market, after officials found chemicals in soybeans, cheered and asked officials to visit more often to help inspect food items to ensure the safety of consumers.
