Ministry of Environment Spokesman: The Royal Government spends $ 900 million every five years to implement climate change prevention projects (Video inside)
(Phnom Penh): Secretary of State and spokesman for the Ministry of Environment, Mr. Net Pheaktra, said that the Royal Government has dropped a budget of about 900 million US dollars every five years to prevent and combat climate change in Cambodia. These budgets have increased fourfold compared to previous years.
In a press conference organized by the Spokesperson of the Royal Government on the morning of August 12, 2020 on "Climate change and the impact on Cambodia" Mr. Net Pheaktra through the budget, the Royal Government has dropped to the Ministry And 14 institutions to work together on climate change.
The spokesman for the Ministry of Environment emphasized that the increase in the budget shows the Royal Government of Cambodia's highest concern in addressing the effects of climate change. He said that climate change has caused a lot of impacts such as floods, droughts, hurricanes and so on.
Mr. Neth Pheaktra said, "In the last eight years, the level of the budget has been increased four times, which means that the Royal Government has paid close attention to addressing the effects of climate change." . "The budget of $ 900 million over five years in the project that we do with the 14 ministries, there are many other sub-projects under this jurisdiction."
According to a spokesman for the Ministry of Environment, the budget addresses the impact. He cited, for example, that if there is a flood that causes damage to roads, then a budget is needed to repair it. Flood-affected people who suffer from drought and damage to rice crops need to use these funds to pump water or other expenses to help them.
At the same time, Mr. Neth Pheaktra added that the budget will be implemented every five years, in which the Royal Government also called for the participation of other development partners. The climate change adaptation project will continue until 2030.
Ministry of Environment spokesman Net Pheaktra also explained that climate change is caused by two factors:
According to Mr. Neth Pheaktra, Cambodia has been severely affected by climate change, with some areas receiving heavy rainfall, causing floods, others with low rainfall, causing drought. Increased sea level causes infiltration of salt water into freshwater systems. Ministry of Health trains mental health care during Kovid-19 outbreak to Battambang provincial health officials
(Battambang): The Department of Mental Health and Substance Abuse of the Ministry of Health organized three training sessions on mental health care during the Kovid-19 epidemic crisis to 96 health officers of the administrative district. 06 in Battambang province.
The two-day training course was held on August 11-12, 2020 under the chairmanship of Ms. Chou Phallyka, Deputy Director of the Department of Mental Health and Substance Abuse of the Ministry of Health, and Dr. Voeun Bun Reth, Director of the Battambang Provincial Health Department. And Dr. Yil Daravuth, WHO Technical Officer for Cambodia.
"This training plays an important role for front-line health officials in rescuing people, combined with mental health care during the Kovid-19 pandemic," said pharmacist Chou Phallika.
In addition, it will contribute to raising the awareness and professional capacity of front-line health officials in providing basic mental health care and support services to clients who will receive health services at health centers and referral hospitals.
