General knowledge: The most devastating volcanic phenomenon in history
This problem greatly affects the ecosystem in which we live. The global warming caused by the eruption of Tambora has reduced rainfall, crop failure and famine in many parts of the world.
It is difficult to estimate how many people died of starvation, but the death toll may have been at least 1 million in the years following the Tambora eruption. If we include the deaths from the global outbreak of a new type of cholera caused by the unusually cold weather of Tambora, the death toll could be in the tens of millions.
According to experts for the world today, the indirect effects of volcanic eruptions are far more serious than their direct effects. Due to advances in technology, we are able to more accurately predict when volcanoes will erupt. This allows for the evacuation of people and the timely deployment of security measures.
