The tycoon said that the names and photos of the two of them were used by the monk Mil Wen Lim, who lives in Australia, because they knew because they had acquaintances in Australia, they were surprised and asked. He was shocked because the use of his name and photo was unauthorized and in the wrong image and action.
Finally, Tycoon Yin Sovanny and Mr. Chea Mong asked Monk Mil Veng Lim, who is known to be living in Australia, to stop using their names and photos without permission if they did not stop. No, there will be legal action.
(Phnom Penh): Climate change is the main factor leading to the shallow water level of the Mekong River, not because of China's or Laos' hydropower dams on Cambodia alone. This was stated by Mr. Te Navuth, Permanent Vice President of the Mekong National Committee.
In a press conference held by the Royal Government Spokesperson on the morning of August 11, 2020, under the theme "Current Situation of Hydrological Regime in the Mekong and Tonle Sap Basin", Mr. Te Navuth also acknowledged that water retention To generate hydropower is also a contributing factor, but the most influential factor is climate change.
The official explained that the dam does not use water, only to hold water or store water to generate electricity, so in the rainy season the dam has to release some excess water and in the dry season to add water. To meet the demand for electricity.
"Less Mekong water also contributes to hydropower, but climate change is a major factor," he said.
Cambodia, as a member of the Mekong River, will continue to work with other member countries to exchange information with each other, as without data there is no information that can be analyzed or judged to ensure the use of the river. Mekong together. This is according to Mr. Te Navuth.
The Mekong River is about 5,000 kilometers long, passing through six countries: China, Myanmar, Thailand, Laos, Cambodia, Vietnam and flowing into the South China Sea. The Mekong River is the artery of Cambodia. It flows through provinces such as Stung Treng, Kratie, Kampong Cham, Phnom Penh and Prey Veng and ends at the Vietnamese border.
Chan Yutha, Secretary of State and spokesman for the Ministry of Water Resources and Meteorology, said that the Mekong River is very important for Cambodia, especially water for agriculture.
He said the Waiko Irrigation System, which extends from Tbong Khmum, Kampong Cham, Prey Veng and Svay Rieng provinces, is a large Cambodian project covering an area of about 300,000 hectares. Through a small river and then continue to distribute along the canal.
According to a spokesman for the Ministry of Water Resources, when the rainy season arrives, if the water level at Kampong Cham station rises about 10 meters, the water will automatically flow into the Vaiko canal, but in the dry season, a pumping station will be used. This is what Cambodia will take advantage of the Mekong River in the future.
Mr. Chan Yutha also did not forget to say that what Cambodia has received from the Mekong River in the past is that when the Mekong River floods will increase into the Tonle Sap about 40 percent. At that time, the Tonle Sap Lake expands, and when the Tonle Sap Lake expands, it will lead to flooding of the surrounding forest, making some aquatic plants become food for fish.
A senior official of the Ministry of Water Resources also added that the flooding of the Mekong River has also brought sediment, which is fertile land for farmers to grow crops. In addition, the Mekong River floods also kill many other pests that bother farmers.
Another benefit that Cambodia has taken from the Mekong River is the reservoir to fill the rivers and lakes to serve the crops of farmers during the dry season. Khmer Breweries provides 100 bins to 11 schools in Dangkor
(Phnom Penh): Khmer Breweries continued to donate 100 additional bins to 11 other primary schools in Dangkor district, Phnom Penh on the morning of August 11, 2020 to improve the hygiene of the school environment.
Dr. Im Sothearith, Public Relations Director of Khmer Breweries, said that the increase in the number of bins to be provided to the other 11 primary schools is a joint effort of Khmer Breweries with Dangkor District, communities and schools to cultivate hygiene habits. Clean students in primary schools to achieve a clean community environment vision.
On August 6, 2020, Khmer Breweries donated 130 bins to 12 primary schools in Dangkor district to keep them in order.
100 Trash Bins Deployed in Russey Sanh Primary School, Prey Sor Primary School, Tuol Rokar Kot Primary School, Trapeang Primary School, Samdech Hun Sen Primary School, Prey Veng Primary School, Tuol Sambo Primary School, Kampong Kdol Primary School, Hun Sen Sak Primary School, Kraing Pong Primary School Pong Teuk and Srenh Primary School. Ministry of Water Resources Spokesman: Three Floods Threaten Phnom Penh (Video inside)
(Phnom Penh): Secretary of State and spokesman for the Ministry of Water Resources and Meteorology, Mr. Chan Yutha, said that floods from three areas that often threaten Phnom Penh. Water from the three areas is the Mekong River flood, Stung Prek Tnaot flood and heavy rainfall in Phnom Penh.
In the press conference of the Royal Government Spokesperson's Unit on "Current Situation of Hydrological Regime in the Mekong and Tonle Sap River Basin" on the morning of August 11, 2020, Mr. Chan Yutha claimed that so far the flood The Mekong River is no longer a risk because of its strong barrier, the Kob Srov Dam.
Mr. Chan Yutha added that the flooding of Stung Prek Tnaot was also facing the same problems due to the shallow land and narrow riverbanks below. The Ministry of Water Resources and Meteorology is studying to solve this problem through rehabilitation, with Chinese companies ready to start operations early next year.
As for the effects of rainfall, a clear study is needed to expand the drainage system to avoid congestion. He added that this water release system does when it rains to absorb water quickly. This is according to Mr. Chan Yutha, Secretary of State and spokesman for the Ministry of Water Resources.
Mr. Chan Yutha explained that for the water release system in Phnom Penh, it is not possible to build a pumping station at the mouth of the canal, that is, to build a reservoir like at Boeung Trabek station, in order to allow the water to flow faster. Flooded by torrential downpours for hours.
Overall, Spokesman Chan Yutha claimed that the two regional threats had been resolved and were coming to an end soon, while the flood threat from relevant institutions and the Phnom Penh Municipal Administration were working together to address them. .
It should be noted that during heavy rains, some areas in Phnom Penh, especially Phsar Doeum Kor, are always flooded, but after a few hours, the floodwaters recede because the Phnom Penh administration has now prepared and restored the system. Release water by arranging the replacement of drains with larger cross-sections.
