The Blue Hat woman who returned from work in Mali has been placed in the Blue Hat Army at the Blue Hat Army Center in Kampong Speu for several days without being diagnosed with Kovid 19. Come and do Chattali Sak at his house in Takhmao City. When I came to do cataracts at home, the virus suddenly appeared and checked for the virus on August 8.
Fearing that the virus would spread to other families and relatives living under the same roof, about 13 relatives were taken to be tested for Kovid 19, but they did not find the Kovid 19 virus. Although 19 Kovids have not yet been found, the 13 relatives have been taken to the second level of the Cataract Center.
O Vandin, Secretary of State for the Ministry of Health and spokesperson for the Ministry of Health, told Fresh News on August 10, 2020 that through direct interviews between the working group and the Blue Hat women who had contracted Kovid 19. This indicates that there are only four people in the woman's house: parents, brother and sister, and some relatives are visiting her house during this time.
She claimed that during the practice at home, she did not communicate directly with her parents, siblings or relatives, but followed the rules of the Ministry of Health by staying separate, eating separately and always wearing a mask. However, using a shared bathroom, even using a shared bathroom, he was careful.
For those involved in this woman, the Secretary of State of the Ministry of Health confirmed that all health checks were taken and no Kovid 19 virus was found, but also required to continue to be vaccinated.
"So now we have collected samples for testing and the results have confirmed that they got a negative result," she said. However, they need to continue to be monitored, and the medical team continues to monitor their health, and they have been assigned to be responsible and managed by the local authorities, while the Kandal Provincial Health Department is also responsible. Need to monitor their health as well.
In a report by Sithbo commune chief Ou Leng on August 10, 2020, it was claimed that the blue hat woman not only had contact with family members in the house, but also cooked soup for the elderly and distributed money to the children. And made food to give to the monks at Wat Chompou in the morning.
Responding to the report, Ms. O Vandin pointed out that she did not know who the authorities went to question and report with. She confirmed that through direct interviews with the Blue Hat women, it was confirmed that he did not go to communicate with outsiders.
At this point, Ms. O Vandin asked the authorities when working on the 19 cases of Kovid to cooperate closely with officials of the Ministry of Health for information clarity and to avoid creating other problems.
She said the Blue Hat woman, along with 79 other Cambodian Blue Hat forces, returned from a mission in Mali on July 10, 2020. After arriving in Cambodia, they were tested for Kovid 19 but no virus was found.
Although no virus was found, all 80 Cambodian Blue Hat soldiers were stationed at the Kampong Speu Blue Hat Army Center for 14 days. On the 13th day of the operation, four Blue Hat forces were diagnosed with Kovid 19, so another 76 Blue Hat forces who had not been found were required to continue their 14-day operation.
On the 13th day of the reconnaissance, four more Blue Hat soldiers were diagnosed with Kovid, 19 more, and another 72 were recalled for another 14 days. With the discovery of 19 cases of Kovid, the 72 forces were allowed to return home and continue their tutelage at home.
According to Ms. O Vandin, of the 72 Blue Hat soldiers who went to do housework at this house, only one woman in Takhmao City was diagnosed with Kovid 19. After this incident, the other Blue Hat troops had to be re-examined for 19 Kovids and were also taken to the Chattali Sak Center in the Blue Hat Soldier Center in Kampong Speu Province.
Ms. O Vandy said that the symptoms of Kovid 19 are very difficult to diagnose. Therefore, she called on those who require home remedies to adhere to the rules of the Ministry of Health to prevent the spread of the disease to families and neighbors.
