(Foreign): Below are some pictures of the international situation selected for Saturday, August 08, 2020.
SEOUL (Reuters) - The death toll from floods and landslides triggered by heavy rains since last Saturday has risen to 21, according to the South Korean Safety and Disaster Prevention and Control Authority. Eleven people are still missing and seven others are injured. This is according to a release by Xinhua on Saturday, August 8, 2020.
As of Friday, heavy rains continued to spread from central South Korea, including Seoul and surrounding cities, to the southern part of the country. The South Korean government has declared seven cities and districts particularly vulnerable to speeding up government aid.
A total of 3,059 people from 1,853 families have been forced from their homes across South Korea, of which 1,433 have not been able to return to their homes. Not yet. A total of 8,439 hectares of farmland were flooded, with 8,246 cases of property damage, including 3,605 cases of private property and 4,641 cases of public property.
Meanwhile, homes, animal shelters, warehouses and factories were flooded, while roads, railways, bridges and reservoirs were damaged. But 72 percent of the damaged property has already been restored in an emergency.
Thai protesters demand the release of two anti-government activists and warn of more protests
BANGKOK: Dozens of protesters gathered in front of a Bangkok court on Saturday to demand the release of two activists and vowed to continue anti-government protests. This is according to the release by CNA on Saturday, August 08, 2020.
Protesters have recently resumed protests against the Thai government, demanding the dissolution of the government of Prayuth Chan-o-cha, who first came to power through a 2014 military coup. Activists, meanwhile, have claimed that the 2019 general election, the first since the coup, was rigged, with Prayuth preparing to ensure he stays in power.
(Stung Treng): During Kovid 19, the Cambodian Red Cross continues to provide humanitarian aid to 258 more families in 14 villages in 4 communes of Thala Brivat and Borey O'Svay Senchey districts in Stung Treng province. August 8-9, 2020.
The Cambodian Red Cross, with Queen Norodom Monineath Sihanouk, the Queen Mother of Cambodia, as the Honorary President and with the Honorary President Dr. Bun Rany Hun Sen, is always concerned, concerned and seeking to alleviate the suffering of Citizens.
For instance, Samdech Kittipritthbandit Bun Rany Hun Sen has assigned Ms. Pum Chantyny, Secretary General of the Cambodian Red Cross to provide relief assistance to the two locations located in the area of Wat Velovan, Sre Russey Village, Sre Russey Commune, Thala Brivat District. 126 families and in Preah Rumkel village, Preah Rumkel commune, Ou Svay Sen Chey district, a total of 132 families.
On this occasion, Mrs. Pum Chantyny brought the Royal Words and speeches of Her Majesty the Queen Mother of Cambodia, Monineath Sihanouk and Samdech Kittiprittbandit Bun Rany Hun Sen from nostalgia, compassion, love and concern, especially about daily life and People's health.
At the same time, she brought the key message in health care, especially to strictly implement preventive measures against Kovid 19 as recommended by the Ministry of Health.
In addition to the recent outbreak of Kovid 19, there has also been an outbreak of chikungunya virus (CHIK) in 15 provinces, and people should be vigilant about the disease.
The transmission of chickenpox is transmitted from one mosquito to another, so to prevent it, clean the area around the house, eliminate all mosquito nets, especially the clear water on car tires, water bottles, pools and so on. Places can hold water. Sleep under a mosquito net (day and night), wear long clothes close by, do not sit or rest in a dark place where mosquitoes like to live.
If you have any symptoms such as fever (sudden high fever (39 degrees Celsius)), red face, headache, pain under the eyes, sore muscles or joints, body aches, red rashes, etc., hurry to the health center or hospital near you. Most.
At the same time, pregnant women should go for regular prenatal check-ups according to the doctor and to give birth at the hospital or health center for the safety of both mother and child, and please do not use alcohol or drugs to damage. Self-health.
Please be informed that during the humanitarian donation to alleviate the hardships of 258 families, each family received: 30 kg of rice, 1 box of noodles, 10 cans of fish, 1 kit (mosquito net, blanket, sarong, scarf, T-shirt), 2 bars of soap. And the Declaration on Measures to Prevent the Spread of Kovid-19.
At the same time, the Cambodian Red Cross also donated some gifts and money to the monks waiting for the rain at Veluvan pagoda.
