US Secretary of State Mike Pompeo announced Wednesday that the United States will launch a new resolution against Iran, despite opposition from Russia and China. The United States has already drafted a resolution to renew sanctions against Iran.
However, the US resolution needs the support of nine of the 15 members of the Security Council and no veto (VETO) from the five permanent members, such as China or the United States. France, England and Russia. Apart from China, Russia has already stated its veto position against the resolution.
Sanctions on Iran will expire on October 18, 2020, under the terms of the Iran Nuclear Agreement (JCPOA), which was signed in July 2015 during the term of Barack Obama. It has vowed to reduce its nuclear activities in order to ease economic sanctions and other benefits.
Unfortunately, current US President Donald Trump formally withdrew the United States from the agreement in May 2018 and unilaterally imposed sanctions on Tehran. In response to US sanctions, Iran violated several agreements.
The European Union, along with Russia and China, has expressed support for arms sanctions, but their priority is to defend the Iran Nuclear Agreement (JCPOA). Since the US withdrawal, there has been no mechanism to rescue the JCPOA.
