The earliest method of making fireworks was to stuff the ammunition into a bamboo tube and then throw it into the fire, creating a loud explosion. Later, the fireworks evolved further, with paper tubes being used instead of bamboo tubes, and they were no longer thrown into the fire, but paper clips were attached to them to ignite them.
By the 10th century, the Chinese had discovered that they could make bombs out of ammunition. So they attached firecrackers with arrows that they fired at the enemy. During the next 200 years, firecrackers were transformed into missiles that could be fired at enemies without the need for arrows. This technology is used in fireworks events.
Ammunition arrived in Europe in the 13th century. The Europeans used this technology to make weapons, but still retained its original purpose. Fireworks were fired to celebrate the occasion. After arriving in Europe, ammunition and rocket formulas spread to other parts of the world as Europe explored new territories.
The tradition of celebrating fireworks continues to be practiced all over the world to this day, and the country that invented the ammunition, China, is still the largest producer and exporter of fireworks.
