Mr. Hun Manet: Students have the opportunity to study in Japan as a potential resource for Cambodia
(Phnom Penh): On the occasion of inviting and congratulating the 48 successful students who will go to study in Japan in the future, Mr. HUN Manet considered the students who received scholarships to study in Japan. Potential resources for Cambodia to contribute to further economic and social development.
The event was held at Phnom Penh Hotel on August 5, 2020 with the participation of Mr. Heng Sour, Secretary of State of the Ministry of Labor and Vocational Training, JDS Scholarship Winners, MEXT Scholarship Winners and 121 Alumni. People.
Mr. HUN Manet also advised all winning students to make efforts to learn and gain experience from Japan in order to develop Cambodia to be more advanced.
He recommended that 120% of the students study hard, 100% study in the classroom and 20% study to learn more about the outside environment by developing relationships and friendships with other students and taking the opportunity to do so. As Cambodia's ambassador to promote Cambodia's culture as well as civilization as well as Cambodia's rich potential tourist sites.
The JDS Scholarship Student Representatives and the MEXT Scholarship Student Representatives are committed to bringing the good knowledge and experience gained from Japan to contribute to the development of the developing nation under the guiding and shadow path. The peace of Samdech Techo Prime Minister, who has transformed the country from empty hands to equal rights in the region and in the world.
It should be noted that the JDS program scholarships were started by the Japanese government in 2000, a total of 416 people, with two master's and two doctoral degrees each year. Of the 857 students currently living in Japan, the Ministry of Education (MEXT) began offering scholarships by the Japanese government in 1993, a total of more than 1,100, ranging from skilled workers to doctoral students.
(Phnom Penh): The Ministry of Tourism has signed a Memorandum of Understanding (MoU) with the Swiss Confederation of Skills Development (SDP) Phase 2, 2020-2024, which focuses on tourism vocational training to promote training. And develop human resources in the tourism sector, especially training young people, students and staff in the tourism and hospitality sector.
The implementation of this project will be carried out in five target provinces, including Kratie, Stung Treng, Ratanakiri, Mondulkiri and Preah Vihear.
The project will also strengthen the capacity of professional staff of the Ministry of Tourism, both nationally and sub-nationally, to establish human capital for tourism in the field of tourism vocational education and training in the context of tourism destinations, and to contribute to the training of trainers and evaluators. .
Mr. Pak Sokhom said that in order to promote the better implementation of the second phase of the skills development program, the Ministry of Tourism has appointed Ministry officials as co-chairs of the project implementation committee with the Swiss Agency for Development and Cooperation. In which, the representative of SDC (donor) is the chairman and the representative of the Ministry of Labor is also the co-vice president.
(Kampong Chhnang): More than 30 Khmer-Muslim families living in Chong Koh Village and Village 4, Sangkat Khsam, Kampong Chhnang City, on the morning of August 5, 2020, protested to ask the Kampong Chhnang Municipal Authority to suspend the dumping of ancient tombs. Their ancestors are temporarily waiting to be resolved and reconciled.
This case happened because on the evening of August 4, 2020, the road construction team of Kampong Chhnang City Hall poured soil to clear their ancient hills and roads almost without any clear solution.
Separately, Mr. Rem El, a representative of the Khmer-Muslim people who protested, said that this ancient road has existed since 1958, until now, there are burial sites of ancestors may be up to thousands of bodies on each floor. But on the evening of August 4, 2020, the road construction team almost completely destroyed the graves and even removed the soul labels and identities of their ancestral corpses by the people whose ancestors were buried. Here do not know.
Regarding this protest, Mr. Besarum, Deputy Governor of Kampong Chhnang City, who inspected the road construction, said that on the evening of August 4, 2020, there were no protests and on the morning of August 5, If there is a protest from the people, then he will make a team to escape from the disputed land to listen to the request of the people, and he also reported to the mayor and the provincial governor to solve the problem. Touch the above as well.
After the representatives of the Khmer-Muslim people in the fourth village and Chong Koh village met with the head of the mosque, Mr. Kob Fari Phu Chuy I (deputy priest) of the mosque in Chong Koh village said that the solution was as requested. Most Khmer-Muslims ask the provincial authorities to keep the land of the Khmer-Muslim graves intact and pour the soil half a meter above the road.
