(Siem Reap): Planting trees in the Angkor resort is an important contribution to creating a green cover for the environment, creating a cultural landscape, attracting tourists as a wildlife sanctuary, especially to protect the temple from changes in temperature and atmosphere.
In fact, on the morning of August 4, 2020, the APSARA Authority, the Siem Reap Provincial Priest, and the people under the initiative of the Chief Inspector of North Angkor Wat, Preah Charnor Sophorn, Dr. Kong Sopheap Thitpanhya 500 trees in the pagoda.
Dr. Kong Sopheap's visit to the tree plant is to promote green beauty in Angkor with APSARA Authority to cultivate love for the environment for all Cambodian children and contribute to reducing climate change with the world because the tree is The source of life provides fresh air and many benefits in human and animal life.
Not only that, the tree is the place where the Buddha was born, enlightened and Nirvana, and the planting of this tree also has many meritorious deeds.
Mr. Yit Chanda, Deputy Director General of APSARA Authority, said that in addition to conservation work, the APSARA Authority pays high attention to the natural environment, which benefits both the temple and the people. This is a noble activity in which the monks of North Angkor Wat and Buddhists plant 500 luxury trees in the pagoda to raise environmental value at Angkor Wat.
He also urged some people who are dissatisfied with the activities of the APSARA Authority, "Whether angry or hateful, do not destroy the trees planted by the APSARA Authority, because the forest is the most important common property of people, the environment and archeology."
He praised and encouraged monks, people, government institutions, the private sector to continue to plant more trees and clean the environment after planting, especially to take care of the trees that have been planted for everyone.
