The meeting was attended by the leaders of the Mine Action Authority, the President of PMAC and the Chairman of the Mapu Province of the seven provinces, the Senior Minister in charge of Mines and Unexploded Ordnance and Engineering, the Royal Cambodian Armed Forces, representatives of the Army and military representatives of the 3rd, 4th and 5th Divisions.
Mr. Prum Sopheak Mongkul, Secretary-General of the Mines Authority, spoke about Cambodia's fulfillment of its obligations as a State Party to the Ottawa Convention, remnants of war remnants of war on Cambodian territory, and mechanisms to develop a demining plan so that Cambodia can achieve its goals. A landmine-free Cambodia by 2025 in accordance with the National Strategic Plan for Mine Action 2018-2025.
On behalf of Samdech Techo, President of the Mine Authority, the Senior Minister expressed his gratitude and appreciation for the efforts of all relevant institutions, especially the provincial administration, which is directly implemented by Phimak and Mapu.
He added that we all have a common commitment and common goal to contribute to the maintenance of peace, security and economic growth for the country and its people through the clearance of landmines and unexploded ordnance. Out of Cambodian territory.
He added that the purpose of Samdech Techo is to turn mines into safe and developed areas in all areas.
Senior Minister Ly Thuch stated that the Royal Government of Cambodia has set out a clear vision and strategic plan to end the threat posed by landmines and explosive remnants of war in Cambodia. The cleanup is completed as planned in 2025.
At the same time, the Senior Minister called on the authorities at all levels to continue to work closely with the Provincial Mine Action Committee and the Mapu Working Group on the selection of priority minefields in response to the actual needs of the people and economic development. This must be assessed accurately and clearly and with the prior approval of all relevant authorities and institutions.
