(Phnom Penh): Crimes are acts committed by one or more people, and those acts cause unrest, disorder, public order, insecurity in society and so on.
Multiple offenses can occur in many different cases, especially if the perpetrator commits several offenses at the same time or at different times, with the perpetrator committing one offense before the court. The perpetrator has been convicted. In this case, it is considered a duplicate offense that needs to apply the rules of the offense to the offender.
Therefore, the legal awareness program of FRESH NEWS, in collaboration with the Cambodian Lawyers for International Cooperation, would like to raise the topic of "Overlapping Offenses" to the following students:
Pursuant to Article 136 of the Penal Code, it is also stated that "duplicate offenses occur when one offense is committed by one person before that person has been convicted of another offense." .
Duplicate offense refers to a situation in which an individual commits an offense before the individual is finally convicted of another offense, that is, if an individual has committed a first offense and has not yet committed it. There is still a final sentencing decision, yet the person commits the second offense, all offenses of such form and form are known as overlapping offenses.
In the case of overlapping offenses, two general rules are applied to the offender, namely, a single charge and a separate charge. However, in special cases, the Penal Code requires a combination of sentences without having to be considered as a single charge or a separate charge.
Public defamation is a felony punishable by a fine for inciting a minor to consume alcohol, so the court can impose two prison sentences. Two) years and a fine of 4,100,000 (four million one hundred thousand) Riels on the offender.
In summary, duplication is an offense in which a person has already committed an offense and has not yet issued a final court decision, then he or she has committed another offense. Punishment in such cases is the principle of the law has set some rules as mentioned above to apply to the perpetrators as well.
