General knowledge: King Alexander won the war, won almost every battle against short life
When Alexandre was 13 years old, he had the power to subdue a mighty black horse that no one, not even its owner, could subdue. This mighty horse also became Prince Alexander's only royal chariot, which he named "Bussefaulus".
King Philip II invited one of the most famous Greek philosophers, Aristotle, to be a mentor to Prince Alexander, and Aristotle agreed. Aristotle trained the prince in many ways, and the prince tried to understand and gain many skills from this great teacher.
After his father's death by assassination by the enemy, Alexander the Great ascended the throne in Pellana in 336 BC and became the "Emperor of Macedonia" in his lifetime. 20 years old.
Father and Mother once said to Alexander, “In the east there is a great store of riches and riches; Of the God of War. This graceful deity awakened Alexander "must have had the ambition to extend Macedonia's power and territory to the east, towards the Persian Empire."
* Battle of the West
At the end of 332 BC, King Alexander continued to lead his army to the famous Egyptian kingdom, overthrowing the Persian army that had ruled Egypt for hundreds of years.
In Egypt, the inhabitants of the region welcomed the presence of the Allied army because they did not want to be under the brutal rule of the Persians, as they led Alexander to become king of Siva. (Siwah) and gave him the title of "Pharaoh".
Alexander the Great, the new Pharaoh of Egypt, decided to build a new city, naming it "Alexandria" after him, and he planned to develop the new city into a prosperous city. An important international port city to date.
The Battle of Babylon and the Pursuit of Emperor Persia III
After defeating the Spartans, Emperor Alexander continued to raise troops on the eastern shores of the Mediterranean Sea, passing through the kingdoms of Phenicia and Syria, heading for Babylon, the capital of the Persian Empire. But the Persian army also came out to intercept the northern part of present-day Iraq. It was then that the Battle of Gaugamela erupted in 330 BC, causing the Persian army to fail.
After the defeat of the Battle of Gogamela in 330 BC, the Western Empire and the capital of Babylon fell to the emperor Alexander III.
* Battle of the Far East and the end of the Persian Empire
The village of Hidapes continued until 325 BC, and this time Alexander's army of eagles was once again defeated under the immense power of the Indian army of Porez.
Alexander the Great still wanted to invade the Indian Empire while his army was getting tired of war. For the last 15 years, they have never returned to their homeland, and many miss their birthdays in Macedonia and Greece. Yes.
In the battle of Hidapes, Alexander was wounded by the spear of the enemy army and lost many of his relatives and generals. Saddened by this, the army also fell into disrepair, causing Alexander to lose weight.
According to some sources, on the way back from Babylon to Babylon, Alexander the Great suffered from malaria while his body was severely wounded and he could not conquer India. He is very sad.
While resting in Babylon, Alexander the Great became obsessed with alcohol, neglected his government, and refused to be cured of his illness, which resulted in his death on the 10th. June 323 BC at the age of 33.
