(Phnom Penh): The Royal Government of Cambodia is cooperating with the governments of countries in the region and the world, as well as national and international organizations and the private sector to jointly fight human trafficking in Cambodia.
In particular, the Ministry of Social Affairs, Veterans and Youth Rehabilitation, which has been mandated by the Royal Government to protect and provide support services to victims, has actively worked and achieved many achievements in protecting victims of human trafficking. Policies to prevent human trafficking, strengthen cooperation and enhance partnerships.
Despite the Ministry's many achievements, the protection of victims of human trafficking has not yet been fully addressed, especially the provision of services and referrals to victims.
This was stated by Mr. Nhek Vannara, Secretary of State of the Ministry of Social Affairs, Veterans and Youth Rehabilitation, representing Mr. Vong Soth, Minister of Social Affairs, Veterans and Youth Rehabilitation, speaking at the 1st National Consultation Workshop Human Trafficking ”organized on July 30, 2020.
Mr. Nhek Vannara stated that in accordance with the above requirements, the Ministry of Social Affairs has been working closely with the United Nations Action for Combating Human Trafficking (UN-ACT), UN Women, International Jewels and Hope. And Justice, who have provided both technical and financial support in the process of developing guidelines for referring victims of trafficking, especially in the organization of this consultation workshop.
He added that today, a national consultation workshop on the final draft of the Principles on the Referral of Victims of Trafficking was organized with the aim of gathering input and input for revision and finalization of the draft guidelines. Referral of victims of human trafficking.
Mr. Nhek Vannara stated that in order to further strengthen the referral and service delivery of victims of human trafficking, we need to develop specific, participatory guidelines, meaning that Involve stakeholders, including ministries, institutions, national and international organizations, and UN agencies at all stages of document development.
He expressed the hope that all officials will do their best to discuss and provide valuable feedback to this workshop so that the technical team can revise the draft principles more effectively and serve the benefit of the people. Our victims.
