(Phnom Penh): Mr. Sok Touch, President of the Royal Academy of Cambodia, sees that free trade between Cambodia and China is not only a trade cooperation between the two countries, but also an opportunity for China to show its independence. A model for trading to countries around the world as well.
Dr. Sok Touch highlighted this in a roundtable discussion on the benefits of the Cambodia-China Free Trade Agreement organized by the Royal Academy of Cambodia on July 28, 2020 at the Royal Academy of Cambodia.
Mr. Sok Touch said that free trade between Cambodia and China is to support each other in trade, not to help either side. In addition, he sees free trade as an opportunity for China to set an example in doing business with friendly countries.
Mr. Sok Touch also saw that the signing of the FTA also shows Cambodia's strength in relations and trade diversification.
It should be noted that through the meeting between Samdech Techo Hun Sen, Prime Minister of Cambodia and Mr. Li Keqiang, Prime Minister of China in January 2019 in Beijing, it was decided to study the possibility of establishing a Cambodia-China bilateral free trade agreement to expand Deepen trade, investment, services and cooperation to achieve the target of 10 billion US dollars in trade by 2023.
Following this initiative, a negotiating team led by the two countries' Ministries of Commerce began in January 2020. The talks were held three times in a row. Cambodian Minister of Commerce Pan Sosak and Chinese Minister of Commerce Zhong Shan announced the closing of the agreement on July 20.
The agreement was handed over to the governments of the two countries to examine the possibility of finding a suitable time to formally sign. The agreement is expected to be signed in late 2020 or early 2021. If a formal agreement is reached, this will be the first bilateral free trade for Cambodia.
The trade volume between Cambodia and China in 2019 was more than 8 billion US dollars. Of that, Cambodia exported more than 1 billion US dollars to China and China imported more than 7 billion US dollars to Cambodia.
Mr. Sok Sopheak said that after the entry into force of the Cambodia-China Free Trade Agreement, Cambodia's exports to China will increase by about 25 percent.
Mr. Sok Sopheak also said that the goods that Cambodia has agreed to import duty-free to China are mostly minerals, chemicals for industry and plastic products used to make various products. According to Sok Sopheak, the preferential treatment for these goods is to support the production chain in Cambodia.
