China suspends Hong Kong extradition treaty with Canada, Australia and UK after they do so
BEIJING: China on Tuesday announced the suspension of Hong Kong extradition treaties with Canada, Australia and the United Kingdom after the three countries made such a decision. This is according to the release by CGTN on Tuesday, July 28, 2020.
All three countries have expressed concern over a new national security law recently passed for Hong Kong before announcing the suspension of their extradition treaty with Hong Kong. Regarding the actions of Canada, Australia and the United Kingdom, Chinese Foreign Ministry spokesman Wang Wenbin said it was wrong and violated international law and general norms of international relations.
New Zealand on Tuesday also announced the suspension of an extradition treaty with Hong Kong, as Beijing voiced its opposition and urged New Zealand to immediately correct its erroneous decision and assert that China has the right to work. Respond appropriately.
