Following the initiative of Cambodian Prime Minister Samdech Techo Hun Sen and Chinese Premier Li Keqiang in a bilateral meeting in January 2019 in Beijing, discussions on the establishment of a Cambodia-China Bilateral Free Trade Agreement were held. Expand trade, investment, services and cooperation to achieve the target of 10 billion US dollars in trade by 2023.
Negotiations on product classification procedures will be held three times via video between the Ministry of Commerce of Cambodia and China in 2020, and this negotiation will be closed in July. The signing of a historic free trade agreement between Cambodia and China is expected to take place in late 2020 or early 2021 between the two countries' leaders.
A free trade agreement (FTA) is an agreement between countries that sign an agreement. This agreement will be facilitated with tariff barriers, limiting the amount of goods which can be understood as a free market economy or in the sense of a unified economy.
Sok Sopheak, secretary of state at the Ministry of Commerce and chairman of the FTA negotiating team, said the free trade agreement between Cambodia and China would open up greater opportunities for Cambodia to import agricultural and processed agricultural products. China without taxes.
According to Sok Sopheak, about 95 percent of Cambodia's 340 items will be tax-free after the agreement is signed. About 5% of the goods will be exempt from tax in the next 10 years.
Dr. Ky Sereywat, an economic researcher, sees the free trade agreement between Cambodia and China as a blessing for Cambodia, which not only provides trade opportunities. But it is also an opportunity for Cambodia's agricultural and tourism investment.
Ky Sereywat sees free trade with China as another smart move by the Royal Government of Cambodia to establish a new trade pole after the EU. And this is also helping Cambodia to escape the economic pressure of the Western powers.
"It has a lot of potential for us," said the economist. When we do free trade with China, we will have further economic growth. It is an opportunity for Cambodia. ”
For Dr. Sok Touch, President of the Royal Academy of Cambodia and a researcher on trade issues between Cambodia and China, also applauded the achievement of a new policy on trade with China. But he remains concerned that Cambodia's unpreparedness could slow down production and lead to little benefit from free trade.
Mr. Sok Touch said that a number of relevant ministries must work together to boost Cambodia's production in response to the FTA. According to Mr. Sok Touch, the relevant ministries that need to strengthen work to increase production include: the Ministry of Commerce, the Ministry of Education, Youth and Sports, the Ministry of Labor and Vocational Training and the Ministry of Agriculture, Forestry and Fisheries.
Mr. Sok Touch also raised a number of other challenges that make it difficult for Cambodia to take advantage of free trade. Challenges include physical infrastructure, production facilities and higher transport costs than neighboring countries, a lack of human resources in heavy and medium industries and a lack of modern technology.
Mr. Sok Touch also advised to be careful with goods from neighboring countries that are labeled in Cambodia and exported to China. According to Sok Touch, doing so would lead to serious losses for Cambodia.
Trade volume between Cambodia and China in 2018 reached about 7.4 billion US dollars, of which Cambodia's exports to China amounted to 1.377 billion US dollars, an increase of 37% and imports from China. $ 6 billion, an increase of 26%.
Mr. Sok Sopheak pointed out that the document of this agreement, Cambodia is superior to China and Cambodia is able to export hundreds of products, all of which are capable of producing and exporting. However, there are some obstacles such as production costs, transportation, tax policy and investment. Dr. Sok Touch, President of the Royal Academy of Cambodia.
