New Zealand announces suspension of extradition treaty with Hong Kong (Video inside) (New Zealand) New Zealand on Tuesday announced that it has decided to suspend extradition treaty with Hong Kong and make some changes. Due to China's recent decision to impose a new national security law on Hong Kong. This is according to the release by CNA on Tuesday, July 28, 2020.
"New Zealand no longer believes that Hong Kong's criminal justice system is independent of China… China's new national security law is abolished," New Zealand Foreign Minister Winston Peters said in a statement. "Principles of law that undermine the principles of one country, two systems."
However, the ambassador stressed that if China returns to adhering to the one-country, two-system policy, New Zealand will be able to reconsider the decision.
The new National Security Law for Hong Kong, which was passed to end protests and separatist protests from China in late June 2020, has been strongly condemned by the West, especially the United States and Britain. And even took action against China. Last week, the British side also decided to suspend the extradition treaty with Hong Kong.
