A statement from the Chinese Ministry of Foreign Affairs said: "At 10:00 a.m. on July 27, at the request of the Chinese side, the US Consulate General in Chengdu was closed. "After that, the authorities went to the entrance of the Consulate General and took control of the situation."
Ahead of the official announcement of the Chinese Foreign Ministry, Chinese media reported that the US flag at the Consulate General was flown at half-mast at 6:18 a.m. Monday.
The United States has insisted that China has no right to retaliate, as China had previously considered the closure of its Houston consulate to cover up the failure of the Donald Trump administration's November US presidential election. China has also denied any allegations of using the consulate to spy and steal data from American citizens.
