Murder! Drunk man dies in Phnom Kravanh district, Pursat province
(Pursat): A man was beaten by a suspect with a bamboo stick, causing a brain haemorrhage at the scene after drinking and arguing with the suspect's father, causing injuries.
The incident happened at 7:30 pm on July 21, 2020 in front of the victim's house in O Heng village, Samrong commune, Phnom Kravanh district, Pursat province.
According to the report from the police, the victim was named Sun Rim, male, 41 years old, Khmer, farmer. The suspect is Thou Thun, male, 34 years old, Khmer, farmer, the son of Noeun Thou, male, 62 years old, Khmer, farmer.
The statement added that before the incident, the victim, Sun Rim, asked Noeun Thou to have a drink together at his house. While drinking alcohol, the victim, Sun Rim, argued with Noeun Thou until he violently broke Noeun Thou's right arm and was sent to the Cardamom Mountain Referral Hospital.
After receiving this information, the suspect, Thou Thun, the son of Noeun Thou, went to help his father and hit the victim's head with a bamboo stick, Sun Rim, causing his head to explode and he died immediately at the scene. The body disappears. Authorities said that the reason for the murder with a bamboo stick caused death was due to drunkenness, verbal conflict, causing violence, and the suspect, a child, ran to help.
After the incident, the police conducted an autopsy and measured and handed over the victim's body to his family for a traditional ceremony. Separately, the perpetrators to the authorities have been researching to bring to justice. Rejoice a lot! Inscriptions found at the Tonle Snguot temple are numbered K.1459 in the Cambodian Inscription Inventory.
Mr. Tina, Deputy Director of the International Center for Research and Documentation of Angkor of the APSARA Authority, said that the inscriptions found at the Tonle Snguot temple may be unregistered and have not been seen before. This inscription is an old stone taken from another place to build the Tonle Snguot temple (as a pavement) built during the reign of Jayavarman VII.
Phnom Penh Municipal Court hands over 7th to 12th century sandstone artifacts to the Ministry of Culture
(Phnom Penh): Mr. Taing Sunlay, President of the Phnom Penh Municipal Court, and Mr. Horm Mengse, Deputy President of the Phnom Penh Municipal Court, handed over a number of artifacts to the Director General of the General Department of Heritage, Ministry of Culture and Fine Arts, to be preserved in the National Museum as an ancestral heritage. We Khmers have preserved it for the next generation of Cambodian children.
The handover ceremony was held on the morning of July 21, 2020, attended by Mr. Ouk Sokha, Director General of the General Department of Heritage, Ministry of Culture and Fine Arts, and Major General Ly Lay, Director of the Department of Heritage Protection, Ministry of Interior.
Mr. Taing Sunlay stated that all the artifacts handed over to the General Department of Heritage of the Ministry of Culture and Fine Arts were confirmed by the cultural experts to be made of sandstone during the 7th, 8th, 11th and 12th centuries.
The President of the Court further stated that these valuable artefacts have been confiscated from the perpetrators for a long time, but recently, the Phnom Penh Municipal Court has developed a new building and found the valuables in the warehouse. Request the Ministry of Culture and Fine Arts, a professional institution, to keep it.
"This activity is a good example for other institutions as well as all our people, because in the past, some people did not understand and always secretly kept it, which is an illegal act on the protection of heritage," he said. "National culture."
