Ministry of Public Works and UNDP sign a memorandum of understanding to implement 4 main tasks
Today's MOU signing ceremony is to open a new page of cooperation between the two institutions on public administration reform, especially the promotion of innovation, the development of public sector managers in the field of innovation and promote the modernization of public services. Promote good governance in state institutions.
The two sides agreed to cooperate in four areas:
* Development and implementation of public sector innovation projects
* Curriculum Development in Public Sector Innovation for the Royal School of Administration
* Promoting digital public service delivery and environmental innovation in Cambodian public administration
* Adjustment of some priority public service delivery procedures
It should be noted that UNDP has been cooperating with the Royal Government of Cambodia in strengthening the rule of law and good governance since the inception of the first National Public Administration Reform Program in the 1990s.
In a meeting with farmers in Prey Kabbas district, Takeo province, on July 21, 2020, Samdech Techo Hun Sen reminded a person who evaluated Samdech as a good leader in holding public forums.
Reminiscing about this assessment, Samdech Techo Hun Sen also replied that Samdech did not claim to be a great person, but he only knew that Samdech was a leader who led Cambodia from a killing field country to a developed and leading country. The country has a population of just over 5 million, a country with a population of nearly 16 million.
Samdech Techo Hun Sen is known as one of the most important political figures in Cambodia, who began to lead the resistance movement to overthrow the Khmer Rouge genocidal regime in 1977. After the fall of the Khmer Rouge in 1979, Samdech Techo Hun Sen became another important leader in leading the new administration of the Cambodian state during his tenure as Minister of Foreign Affairs.
Samdech Techo Hun Sen became highly responsible for Cambodia's fate after the Cambodian National Assembly elected him chairman of the State Council or Prime Minister in 1985.
Since then, the leader, nicknamed the Cambodian Strongman, has struggled to lead Cambodia out of a difficult situation by addressing the people's hunger and seeking an end to the war crisis in Cambodia.
This young, new Cambodian leader has consistently succeeded in his politics, both in solving the problem of famine and in resolving the political crisis, until the re-election in 1993 and the end of the war in 1998.
More than 20 years after the end of the flames of war brought a new page in the history of peace and unity, Samdech Techo Hun Sen received full opportunities for national development to push Cambodia from a poor country to a middle-income country. At the current low, the poverty rate was below 10 percent in 2015, maintaining a steady economic growth of around 7 percent over the last two decades.
