Climate change could cause polar bears to become extinct by the end of the century
LONDON (Reuters) - Polar bears could become extinct on Earth by the end of the century, according to a new study. This is according to a quote from the BBC on Tuesday, July 21, 2020.
The study claims that climate change caused by the effects of global warming will cause the polar ice caps to begin to melt. As a result, polar bears that depend on glaciers are losing their habitat and food source.
The study also found that at least two groups of polar bears have already been affected by the melting sea. One community is located off the southeast coast of Alaska, while the other community is found in the Gulf of Hudson, Canada.
This carnivore relies on the Arctic Ocean ice to hunt other animals, such as seals for food. However, as the glaciers melt faster, they are forced to travel long distances or reach shore, where it is difficult to find food, especially for their young.
Polar bears are listed by the International Union for Conservation of Nature (IUCN) as one of the most endangered species due to climate change.
